version: "2.0"
description: >-
  The Warpline Engine — Phase 1: the Convergence/Divergence Oracle. A read-only tool
  that lifts two git branches to a content-addressed store of MEANING (the
  Paradigm symbol graph), predicts the merge from meaning (clean / knot /
  dangling), runs git's actual merge read-only, and scores where meaning and
  bytes agreed or diverged — appending a row to .warpline/oracle.jsonl. Never touches
  HEAD/index/worktree.

components:
  git-exec:
    description: >-
      Thin, READ-ONLY wrappers over git (merge-base, rev-parse, commit meta,
      merge-tree). ABSORB materializes a ref's tree via `git archive | tar` into a
      temp dir — NO worktree, no .git/worktrees lock — so absorbs run concurrently
      (T-2026-06-23-003). worktreeAdd/Remove remain only for the cold git<2.38
      merge-tree fallback, which needs real merge machinery and is serialized
      per-repo via #repo-lock. The user's HEAD/index/worktree are never touched.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#repo-lock"

  repo-lock:
    description: >-
      A per-repo, in-process async mutex (withRepoLock(repoRoot, fn)). Serializes
      the one remaining git-worktree user — the cold git<2.38 merge-tree fallback —
      per resolved repo root so concurrent calls can't race the .git/worktrees lock;
      distinct repos run free. A predecessor's rejection never blocks later callers.
      In-process only (cross-process would need a .git flock; the parallel-absorb
      path no longer needs a worktree at all).
    type: module

  canonical-json:
    description: >-
      Strict, dependency-free canonical serializer (sorted keys, NFC strings, no
      whitespace ambiguity) that produces the byte stream the essence hash digests.
    type: module

  essence-hash:
    description: >-
      The load-bearing wall — essence(symbol) -> contentId. Hashes the Canonical
      Normal Form ⟨kind, normalized contract, edgeBag⟩, Merkle by TARGET essence
      (so rename is free), excludes name/path/prose/timestamps. Tarjan SCC for
      cycles; stable-id fold for generic-contract aliasing. .purpose contentId =
      "essence:v0:" + sha256(canonical). STAGE 3a: a code-unit branch in localCNF
      (detected by componentType==='code-unit') replaces the sorted edgeBag with
      the CCNF body and SUBSTITUTES each positional f:N token INLINE with the
      essence of codeLocalTargets[N]'s edge (via the SAME edgeEssence resolver, so
      intra-SCC targets become @scc-internal and a cross-symbol rename frees the
      chain); extern/builtin free:name tokens stay untouched; edgeBag is []. The
      version tag is read off entry.data.essenceTag (default ESSENCE_VERSION) so a
      code-unit contentId is "essence:v1:ts<exact>:"+sha256(canonical) and SCC
      members carry the same tag — mixed v0/v1 graphs interoperate because Merkle
      substitutes the target's full TAGGED string. essence-hash never imports the
      lens; the tag travels on the node.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#canonical-json"
      - "#warp-object"

  warp-object:
    description: >-
      A WarpObject — a SymbolEntry stripped of labels and reduced to ⟨kind,
      contract, sorted outgoing edges, contentId⟩. liftEdges derives outgoing,
      typed, deduped, sorted edges from the live SymbolIndex (the single edge
      source the essence hash agrees on).
    type: module

  warp-state:
    description: >-
      A WarpState — the whole symbol graph of one ref lifted to MEANING. stateId =
      sha256 over the sorted contentIds; treeSha/absorbedAt are provenance, not
      identity. WarpObject.filePath is normalized REPO-RELATIVE to the absorb root
      (temp-worktree or cwd, with macOS /private↔/var alias handling) so a
      path-prefix difference never masquerades as a rename.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#warp-object"
      - "#essence-hash"

  warp-store:
    description: >-
      Minimal content-addressed store (in-mem primary, .warpline/ disk debug cache)
      and the .warpline/oracle.jsonl ledger appender. The ONLY thing Warpline writes to
      disk is under .warpline/.
    type: module

  code-lens:
    description: >-
      The TS code-lens interface surface (spec §2 / warpline-code-lens.md). Types
      ONLY (stage 1): CodeUnit — a SymbolEntry-shaped synthetic node keyed
      #code:<rel-path>::<qualified-name>, carrying the identity-bearing
      codeEssence (CCNF) body slot, resolved free-reference edges, and a visible
      reducedFidelity marker — and the CodeLens registry contract (extensions +
      lift()). Declares no behavior; pins the shape the lift machinery (ts-lens,
      stage 2) is built against. componentType 'code-unit' lets code-units flow
      through the EXISTING WARP (essence-hash/sem-delta/predict/oracle) as nodes
      in the same universe, never a parallel graph.
    type: module

  code-essence:
    description: >-
      The pure-syntactic Code Canonical Normal Form (CCNF) producer — codeCNF(node)
      -> canonical token string for one declaration. The SYNTACTIC HALF of the
      lens: structure + WRITTEN annotations only, NO ts.Program/checker. Hardens
      the AST-absorb spike: (1) scope-correct alpha-normalization honoring JS
      hoisting (var/fn-decl -> nearest function scope; let/const/class -> block;
      params/type-params -> unit scope; catch var -> catch scope) with bindings as
      (depth, ordinal) coordinates and shadowing-correct use resolution, replacing
      the spike's unsound flat first-declaration-order map; (2) separate b:/t:/L:
      namespaces (the spike ignored type-params, so f<T> vs f<U> falsely DIFFERed);
      (3) the false-EQUAL guard (§3.2) — async/generator*/static/readonly/abstract/
      get-set/optional?/default-presence/rest.../definite!/optional-chain?./nullish??
      each produce a DISTINCT cnf; (4) literal normalization (§3.3) — integers
      (hex/oct/bin) -> exact decimal, bigint -> decimal+n, non-integer numerics ->
      raw source lexeme NOT an IEEE-754 round-trip (0.1 != 1e-1 by design),
      strings/templates NFC; (5) directive comments (@ts-ignore/@ts-expect-error/
      @ts-nocheck/eslint-disable) hashed, free prose dropped. An optional
      freeRefClassifier strips free names to positional f:{idx} edge tokens (default
      emits free:{name}, keeping stage 1 self-contained and spike-equivalent).
      Stage 2 adds codeCNFDetailed(node,opts) -> {cnf, freeRefs[]}: the SAME
      first-appearance ordering drives both the body's f:{idx} tokens and the
      freeRefs index, so freeRefs[idx] (name + a representative ts.Identifier node)
      aligns 1:1 with the f:idx token. codeCNF now delegates to it and returns .cnf
      (the stage-1 signature is unchanged). This positional alignment is the input
      the lens hands the checker for §4 resolution; call order is meaning, so a
      sorted edge-set would be wrong.
    type: module

  code-symbol:
    description: >-
      Pure string builders for a code-unit's WARP key and structural stable key
      (spec §2). codeSymbol(relPath, qualifiedName) -> "#code:"+relPath+"::"+
      qualifiedName (the SymbolIndex key); codeStableKey(relPath, structuralPath)
      -> relPath+"::"+structuralPath where structuralPath is the (scopeKind#ordinal)
      chain (e.g. class#0/method#2). Both are LABELS — provenance + rename
      tiebreakers — NEVER hashed; pure functions of their inputs (no I/O), so
      trivially deterministic across machines.
    type: module

  ts-lens:
    description: >-
      The TS/TSX code-lens (stage 2, spec §4/§5) — the checker-dependent half.
      TsLens.lift(rootDir) enumerates .ts/.tsx files (sorted, .d.ts excluded),
      builds ONE ts.Program over them against a FIXED, HARDCODED compiler-options
      baseline (ESNext target/module, Bundler resolution, strict, skipLibCheck,
      noEmit, allowJs:false — NEVER a discovered tsconfig, §5.2), and lifts each
      code-unit (FunctionDeclaration, arrow/fn-expr const, class + its method/ctor/
      getter/setter/field members; default-export -> qualifiedName 'default';
      overload sets -> the implementation signature, bodiless overloads skipped).
      Per unit it computes codeEssence via codeCNFDetailed and resolves each free
      VALUE ref to one of four §4 classes, ALIGNED to the body's f:idx slots: (a)
      module-local lifted code-unit -> 'local' edge (edgeKind calls/reads) carrying
      the target #code: symbol (body emits f:idx); (b) imported name -> 'extern' id
      extern:<specifier-as-written>#<exportName> derived from the SYNTACTIC import
      statement, NOT checker module resolution (§5.3 — deterministic with no
      node_modules); (c) builtin/global -> 'builtin' name; (d) unresolved -> set
      reducedFidelity. Checker is used for identity resolution ONLY (getSymbolAtLocation
      + getAliasedSymbol), never inferred types (§5.1). lift() returns CodeUnit[]
      sorted by symbol (determinism). Exports TS_LENS_VERSION (the exact pinned ts
      version) for stage 3 to stamp into the essence tag.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#code-essence"
      - "#code-symbol"
      - "#code-lens"

  lens-registry:
    description: >-
      The code-lens registry (spec §9). lensFor(ext) -> CodeLens | undefined maps a
      file extension (incl. leading dot) to the responsible lens; v1 registers only
      TsLens (.ts/.tsx). The single seam future per-language lenses plug into without
      touching the lift/absorb path.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#code-lens"
      - "#ts-lens"

  lift-code-units:
    description: >-
      The absorb-side bridge (stage 3a, spec §9 sub-phase 2 / §4.1).
      liftCodeUnits(rootDir) runs every REGISTERED lens over a read-only worktree
      root and collects the union of CodeUnits (re-sorted by symbol for
      deterministic injection order). injectCodeUnits(index, units) turns each
      CodeUnit into a synthetic SymbolEntry (kind 'component' + componentType
      'code-unit'; DETERMINISTIC id = sha256('code:'+symbol), NOT a uuid; source
      'premise'; references = the distinct LOCAL targets in first-appearance order
      so liftEdges yields code->localTarget edges into buildEssenceGraph.adj and
      Tarjan detects code cycles) and splices it into the live SymbolIndex
      (entries + byType + bySource) BEFORE computeEssences runs — same universe,
      never a parallel graph. The identity-bearing payload travels on entry.data:
      codeEssence (body with positional f:idx slots), codeLocalTargets (the
      ordered distinct local target symbols the f:N slots substitute against), and
      essenceTag = "v1:ts"+TS_LENS_VERSION (the compiler-pinned version namespace
      §5.2, so essence-hash never imports the lens — the tag travels with the node).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#code-lens"
      - "#lens-registry"
      - "#ts-lens"

  absorb:
    description: >-
      absorb(gitRef) -> WarpState. Materializes the ref's tree via `git archive |
      tar` into a temp dir (NO worktree, no .git/worktrees lock — concurrency-safe,
      T-2026-06-23-003), loadLiveGraph on it, lifts every symbol, builds the
      WarpState, releaseTree in a finally. The literal WORKTREE ref absorbs the live
      cwd uncommitted state.
      STAGE 3a: after loadLiveGraph and before buildWarpState (both the ref and
      WORKTREE paths), liftCodeUnits + injectCodeUnits splice TS/TSX code-units
      into the index as synthetic nodes so code essences flow through the same
      WARP. The lens only READS the worktree/cwd — the read-only invariant holds.
      DETERMINISM INVARIANT: absorbing the same ref twice is byte-identical
      (stateId + every per-symbol contentId + repo-relative filePath), so
      diff(absorb(HEAD), absorb(HEAD)) is provably ZERO. Rests on (a) the absorb
      root being passed to buildWarpState for repo-relative filePaths, (b)
      deterministic .purpose/portal file-discovery order in premise-core's
      aggregator, and (c) the lens's sorted file enumeration + deterministic
      synthetic ids (duplicate-symbol resolution + Merkle essences are stable
      across parses regardless of filesystem order or temp-dir prefix).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#git-exec"
      - "#warp-state"
      - "#lift-code-units"

  sem-delta:
    description: >-
      The SemDelta and diff(base, branch), keyed by stableKey (SymbolEntry.id, so
      rename survives). contentId-equal short-circuits to no-delta; a pure
      rename/move is the EMPTY delta. Classifies changed contract slots and edge
      add/remove. A symbol-born delta carries an edgesAdded-only changeset (ALL of
      the born object's outgoing edges) so a new symbol's outgoing edges feed the
      dangle pass the same way a gained edge does (born-consumer → retired-target).
      STAGE 3b: ContractChangeset gains a bodyChanged flag → a 'body' slot in
      changedSlotsOf. classify sets bodyChanged when either side is a code-unit:
      classify runs ONLY when contentIds already differ, and a code-unit's identity
      IS its body (incl. inline-substituted local refs), so a differing code-unit
      contentId means the body moved. 'body' is the body-INTERNAL analog of 'steps'
      (a literal/operator change with no call change fires 'body' but not 'edges'; a
      call add/remove still fires 'edges' via the existing path — both correct). This
      closes the TD-806 "0 deltas on a .ts-only change" gap.
    type: module

  predict:
    description: >-
      predict(ΔA, ΔB) -> {autoClean, knots[], dangling[]}. The commute / knot /
      dangle decision procedure. Dangle wins precedence so the score is a true
      partition; same-key identical-essence is convergent (autoClean). STAGE 3b:
      'body' joins the scalar-conflict-slot list (componentType|kind|steps|body) so
      two branches that edit the SAME code-unit's body to DIFFERENT essences are a
      KNOT with conflictingSlots ['body'] (git may auto-merge textually-distant
      edits Warpline flags at function granularity); a call to a code-unit the other
      branch retired surfaces as a DANGLE via the unchanged edge pass.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#sem-delta"

  justification:
    description: >-
      The v0 Justification — actor/intent from the branch tip's git log,
      computedRipple via getReferencesTo over changed symbols, danglingRefs, and a
      reserved signature field (unsigned:sha256 stub).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#git-exec"

  oracle:
    description: >-
      oracle(branchA, branchB) — merge-base → absorb base/A/B → diff → predict →
      git merge-tree reality (read-only) → score the confusion matrix
      (agreeClean/agreeConflict/divergeGitOnly/divergeMeaningOnly/score/verdict) →
      append an OracleRecord to .warpline/oracle.jsonl. The two divergence cells are
      the experimental result. Conflict paths map to symbols at .purpose-dir level;
      a git conflict in a NON-SYMBOL file (or an unresolvable merge) becomes
      gitConflictUnmapped — a git-only divergence that gates the verdict, so the
      oracle NEVER reads CONVERGENT over a real git conflict (T-2026-06-25-001). The
      ACTIONABLE answer is a separate mergeClean flag (no knots ∧ no dangling ∧ no git
      conflict); convergence measures meaning⟷git AGREEMENT, so a contested merge both
      sides predict scores 'convergent agreement' but is NOT mergeClean — read
      mergeClean for "is it safe to weave?" (T-2026-06-25-005).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#absorb"
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#predict"
      - "#justification"
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#git-exec"

  weave:
    description: >-
      The pre-merge MEANING forecast + the semantic-diff report — READ-ONLY and,
      unlike #oracle, never writes .warpline/. forecast(A, B) reuses the Oracle
      pipeline up through predict() (mergeBase → absorb base/A/B → diff → predict)
      and surfaces it as pre-merge UX: a CLEAN-vs-DECISIONS verdict, autoClean
      count, knots (symbol + contending sides), dangling (from→target, retiredBy).
      An optional vsGit flag also runs the full Oracle (git merge-tree) read-only
      for the meaning-vs-bytes divergence comparison. semanticDiff(refA, refB)
      groups the SemDeltas (born / retired / contract-changed-with-slots /
      renamed-noop) so the "rename/move is the EMPTY delta" property is visible on
      real refs. The `weave` WRITE verb is RESERVED, not implemented in Phase 1.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#absorb"
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#predict"
      - "#oracle"
      - "#git-exec"

  consolidate:
    description: >-
      The N-way fold FORECAST (read-only) — the N-branch generalization of #weave's
      forecast(). consolidate(refs[], {base?}) absorbs a common base + N refs IN
      PARALLEL (concurrency-safe via git-archive materialization, T-2026-06-23-003),
      diffs each against base, and runs the proven 2-way #predict PAIRWISE across
      every (i<j), aggregating by symbol: an N-way knot is the union of the pairwise
      knots on a stableKey (sides = the refs in tension), a dangle is an edge one ref
      added into a symbol another ref retired, autoFolded = touched keys with no
      decision. decisions (K_human) = knots + dangles. NO git merge, NO ledger write.
      The engine behind the falsifiable multi-agent claim (T-2026-06-24-012): a
      meaning-preserving edit (reorder, local rename) carries ZERO delta and
      auto-folds where git textually conflicts, so human labor tracks GENUINE
      meaning-collisions, not branch count. Engine only — no CLI verb wired yet.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#absorb"
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#predict"
      - "#git-exec"

  lifeline:
    description: >-
      Meaning-aware blame — lifeline(symbol, {maxCommits}) traces a symbol's ESSENCE
      through the commits that touched its file (git log --follow, so the thread
      survives the renames/moves git blame resets on), attributing each essence-change
      to the commit that INTRODUCED it (newest-first) with the author + INTENT (the
      commit subject git blame can't show). Resolves the symbol at HEAD, absorbs up to
      maxCommits of the file history IN PARALLEL (concurrency-safe per T-2026-06-23-003),
      and emits ONLY genuine meaning changes — a commit that touched the file but not
      the symbol's meaning is NOT an event. Read-only. MVP: tracks by the ::qualified
      suffix (rename-stable across file moves; a same-suffix collision elsewhere is a
      known limitation).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#absorb"
      - "#git-exec"

  warpline-cli:
    description: >-
      The warpline command line (commander). `warpline oracle <a> <b> [--json]`,
      `warpline absorb <ref> [--json]`, `warpline weave --preview <a> <b> [--vs-git]
      [--json]` (the pre-merge forecast; the bare `weave` write verb is reserved
      and refuses with a read-only notice), `warpline consolidate <refs...> [--base R]
      [--json]` (the N-way fold forecast — CLEAN-to-fold vs N decisions, knots with
      per-ref sides, dangling, auto-fold count), and `warpline diff [refA] [refB]
      [--json]` (semantic diff; no args = WORKTREE vs HEAD, one arg = ref vs HEAD,
      two args = refA vs refB). Plus `warpline status [--json]` (the working tree's
      MEANING vs HEAD — the named, discoverable form of diff-no-args) and `warpline
      lifeline <symbol> [--max N] [--json]` (meaning-aware blame — who changed a
      symbol's meaning, when, why; survives renames). The only module allowed to
      write stdout; library code stays quiet.
    type: cli
    components:
      - "#oracle"
      - "#absorb"
      - "#weave"
      - "#consolidate"
      - "#lifeline"

flows:
  oracle-run:
    description: >-
      The end-to-end Oracle flow: merge-base, absorb base/A/B, diff each side,
      synthesize justifications, predict the merge from meaning, run git's real
      merge read-only, score the confusion matrix, append the OracleRecord.
    steps:
      - component: "#git-exec"
        action: "Resolve merge-base(A, B)"
      - component: "#absorb"
        action: "Lift base, A, B to WarpStates (detached temp worktrees)"
      - component: "#sem-delta"
        action: "diff(base, A) and diff(base, B)"
      - component: "#justification"
        action: "Synthesize justA / justB from git log + ripple"
      - component: "#predict"
        action: "predict(ΔA, ΔB) → autoClean / knots / dangling"
      - component: "#git-exec"
        action: "git merge-tree --write-tree A B (read-only) for the real conflict set"
      - component: "#oracle"
        action: "Score the confusion matrix and append the OracleRecord to .warpline/oracle.jsonl"

  absorb-flow:
    description: >-
      One ref -> a WarpState of MEANING (stage 3a). Materialize a read-only ref
      tree (git archive | tar, no worktree), live-parse it, lift+inject TS/TSX
      code-units as synthetic nodes in the SAME universe, then compute whole-state
      essences and stamp every object. The lens only reads the tree — the Phase-1
      read-only invariant holds.
    steps:
      - component: "#git-exec"
        action: "git archive | tar -x materialize the ref into a temp dir (no worktree, lock-free, read-only)"
      - component: "#lift-code-units"
        action: "liftCodeUnits(worktree) then injectCodeUnits(index, units) — synthetic code-unit nodes spliced in BEFORE essence computation"
      - component: "#essence-hash"
        action: "computeEssences over the merged .purpose+code universe (code-units take the v1:ts<exact> tag, .purpose stays v0)"
      - component: "#warp-state"
        action: "buildWarpState stamps each object's contentId and derives the stateId; remove the worktree in a finally"

  weave-forecast:
    description: >-
      The pre-merge MEANING forecast (read-only, ephemeral — no ledger write).
      Reuses the Oracle pipeline up through predict() only; git is consulted just
      when --vs-git is requested.
    steps:
      - component: "#git-exec"
        action: "Resolve merge-base(A, B)"
      - component: "#absorb"
        action: "Lift base, A, B to WarpStates (detached temp worktrees)"
      - component: "#sem-delta"
        action: "diff(base, A) and diff(base, B)"
      - component: "#predict"
        action: "predict(ΔA, ΔB) → autoClean / knots / dangling → CLEAN vs DECISIONS verdict"
      - component: "#weave"
        action: "Optionally (--vs-git) run the full #oracle read-only for the meaning-vs-bytes divergence"

aspects:
  ~read-only:
    description: >-
      THE PHASE-1 SAFETY INVARIANT — Warpline NEVER mutates the user's git
      HEAD/index/worktree. A ref is read by materializing its tree via `git archive`
      into a throwaway temp dir (no worktree, no .git/worktrees lock); the cold
      git<2.38 merge-tree fallback uses a detached worktree torn down in a finally.
      The ONLY disk Warpline writes is under .warpline/ (the content-addressed store
      + the oracle.jsonl ledger). Lifting a ref, or the WORKTREE pseudo-ref, only
      READS files — the code-lens included.
    category: invariant
    severity: critical
    tags: [safety, read-only, git, phase-1]
    anchors:
      - src/git/git-exec.ts:1-16
      - src/git/git-exec.ts:128-180
      - src/absorb.ts:37-66
      - src/git/repo-lock.ts:1-22
      - src/warp/store.ts:53-64
    applies-to:
      - "#git-exec"
      - "#repo-lock"
      - "#absorb"
      - "#consolidate"
      - "#lifeline"
      - "#lift-code-units"
      - "#ts-lens"
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#oracle"
      - "#weave"

  ~determinism:
    description: >-
      THE THESIS — absorbing the same ref twice is byte-identical (stateId + every
      per-symbol contentId + repo-relative filePath), so diff(absorb(HEAD),
      absorb(HEAD)) is provably ZERO and two machines on the same source agree. No
      timestamps/paths/positions/prose/embeddings enter the hashed byte stream;
      every set is sorted+deduped; strict canonical JSON; sorted file enumeration;
      deterministic synthetic code-unit ids; the TS compiler is exact-pinned and
      stamped into the code essence tag (essence:v1:ts<exact>) so a different
      compiler is a different content-address namespace, never a silent collision.
    category: invariant
    severity: critical
    tags: [thesis, determinism, hashing, reproducibility]
    anchors:
      - src/warp/warp-state.ts:106-111
      - src/lens/ts-essence.ts:374-393
      - src/absorb.ts:1-14
      - test/absorb.test.ts:54-77
    applies-to:
      - "#essence-hash"
      - "#canonical-json"
      - "#code-essence"
      - "#ts-lens"
      - "#warp-state"
      - "#absorb"
      - "#consolidate"

  ~meaning-coverage:
    description: >-
      THE TD-806 HONESTY METRIC — Warpline reports WHERE it has meaning versus
      where it degrades to opaque bytes (the 3-tier fidelity model). A code-unit the
      TS lens cannot soundly lift (unresolved refs, unsupported forms) is flagged
      reducedFidelity — never silently empty; coverage is surfaced, not assumed.
      Honest about its own blind spots so the meaning layer's thickness is legible.
    category: rule
    severity: medium
    tags: [honesty, fidelity-tiers, td-806, coverage]
    anchors:
      - src/lens/ts-lens.ts:391-431
      - src/lens/lift-code-units.ts:120-135
    applies-to:
      - "#lift-code-units"
      - "#ts-lens"
      - "#code-lens"
